        <div id="cost-per-25g" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cost per 25 g of protein is the number that makes Paleovalley hard to recommend as a main protein source. A 30-serving tub at about 13 g per scoop holds roughly 390 g of protein. At the $50 to $70 range we saw across the brand, Amazon and partner retailers, that is about $3.20 to $4.50 for every 25 g. Prices move with promotions and subscriptions, so treat those figures as approximate.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers (approximate)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">13 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">30</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Scoops per tub</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~390 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per tub</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$3.20+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g of protein</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For context, mainstream whey usually lands somewhere near $1 per 25 g and budget tubs lower still. Even within bone broth, rivals with bigger scoops come out cheaper: Ancient Nutrition's Pure flavor lists about 20 g per scoop and the larger single-ingredient tubs from smaller brands get well under Paleovalley's cost per gram. Paleovalley is the premium end of an already premium category.
          </p>

          <p>
            The way to make the price sensible is to use less of it, for a different job. One scoop a day in coffee or soup costs roughly $1.70 to $2.30 at typical prices and adds about 13 g of protein and some collagen to a food you would eat anyway. That is a reasonable spend for a lot of people. Two scoops a day as a post-workout shake is where the economics break down.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want to compare honestly, divide the tub price by the total grams of protein, then multiply by 25. Do the same for any rival. That one calculation, which our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">cheapest protein powder guide</a> runs across brands, settles most protein powder debates faster than any marketing claim on the front of the tub.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="collagen-vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Bone Broth Protein vs Whey for Muscle
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bone broth protein is mostly collagen, and collagen is an unusual protein. It is rich in glycine, proline and hydroxyproline, the amino acids that give connective tissue its structure, but it is low in leucine and contains essentially no tryptophan. That makes it an incomplete protein by the standard definition, which is why it scores poorly on protein quality measures compared with whey, egg, soy or a well-built plant blend.
          </p>

          <p>
            For muscle, leucine is the amino acid that matters most, because it acts as the signal that switches on muscle protein synthesis after a meal. A 25 g scoop of whey carries roughly 2.5 to 3 g of leucine. A scoop of bone broth protein carries a small fraction of that. Two scoops of Paleovalley still would not match one scoop of whey for this purpose, so a shake made only from broth powder is a poor post-workout choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not make bone broth protein useless. Collagen protein still counts toward your daily protein total, and research on collagen peptides has looked at skin, joint and tendon outcomes with mixed but sometimes encouraging results. The honest framing is that it does a different job. Our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen protein powder guide</a> covers what that research does and does not show.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical answer for most people is to use both. Get your main protein doses from food and, if you use powder, a complete source like whey or a soy or pea blend. Add a scoop of bone broth protein on top if you like it in coffee or soup. If you are not sure how much protein you need in the first place, our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a> walks through the numbers by body weight and goal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Testing, Sourcing Claims and What Is Missing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paleovalley says it tests every batch for more than 40 pesticides and herbicides, and retailers that carry the powder repeat that claim. That is a meaningful step for a product sold on the strength of grass-fed sourcing, because pasture-raised cattle can still be exposed to sprayed land nearby. We could not find a published certificate on the pages we checked, so if those results matter to you, ask the brand for the current batch report before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the powder does not carry, as far as we could confirm, is a sport certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. Those programs screen for banned substances and are mainly relevant to drug-tested athletes. Bone broth protein is an unlikely place for a stimulant or steroid contaminant to turn up, but if you compete under drug testing, the rule is simple: use only certified products and check the certifier's database.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are the other question people ask. Bones store minerals, including small amounts of lead, so broth products are reasonable to test for metals. We could not verify specific heavy metal figures for Paleovalley from a primary source, so we are not repeating claims made about it secondhand. A brand that runs pesticide panels will usually share metals data on request, and it is worth asking.
          </p>

          <p>
            The grass-fed claims are the brand's own, and there is no third-party grass-fed certification shown on the listings we checked. That is common in the category. Paleovalley has built its whole business on sourcing and has every reason to protect that reputation, which counts for something, but buyers who want a certified claim should know it is self-reported. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains how to weigh testing claims across brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip Paleovalley if your goal is muscle gain or you need a large protein dose from one shake. The small, collagen-heavy scoop is the wrong tool, and you will spend three to four times as much per gram as you would on a whey isolate. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">whey protein isolate picks</a> or a pea and rice blend will do far more for your training for far less money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are on a tight budget. Even used once a day, the tub runs out in a month, and there are bone broth powders with bigger scoops for less. If you like the idea of beef protein but want better numbers, our <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">beef protein powder guide</a> compares hydrolyzed beef isolates that deliver more protein per scoop, though they are processed differently.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip the flavored versions if you are allergic to coconut, and check with a doctor before using any concentrated protein or broth powder if you have kidney disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, or are on a sodium-restricted diet. Broth naturally carries sodium and potassium, and both matter for some medical conditions. This review is not medical advice, and a registered dietitian can tell you how a product fits your situation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, skip it if you want a powder that dissolves instantly in cold water. Because it is a dried broth rather than an acid-hydrolyzed collagen, the brand itself notes it does not mix as completely as commercial collagen, and buyer feedback echoes that. It does best in warm liquids, blenders and recipes. If that sounds like a chore, a hydrolyzed collagen peptide powder is easier to live with.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Use It Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best use is the one the brand suggests: one scoop in 8 to 12 fl oz of a warm or cold drink. Coffee is the most popular choice in buyer reviews, and the Chocolate and Vanilla flavors are built for it, adding sweetness from monk fruit without sugar. Stir it into hot liquid rather than shaking it cold, and use a small frother or blender if you dislike any residue.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unflavored is the most versatile tub because it works in savory food. Stir a scoop into soup, stew, sauce or a mug of hot water with salt and pepper, and it behaves like a concentrated broth. Buyers also add it to oatmeal and baked goods, where the brand describes the flavor as mild and slightly nutty. In baking, swap it for a small share of the flour rather than expecting it to behave like whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pair it with a complete protein when you can. A coffee with a scoop of Paleovalley next to a breakfast of eggs or Greek yogurt is a sensible way to combine its collagen with the leucine that makes a meal count for muscle. Using it alongside food also means the small scoop is a top-up rather than the whole meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the tub sealed and dry, because broth powder clumps in humid kitchens. Use a dry scoop every time and store the tub away from the kettle. If you buy on subscription to bring the price down, set the interval to match your real use. At one scoop a day, a 30-serving tub lasts exactly a month, which is useful for budgeting the real monthly cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Paleovalley vs Ancient Nutrition vs Naked Bone Broth
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ancient Nutrition is the brand most shoppers compare Paleovalley with. Its Bone Broth Protein Pure lists about 20 g of protein in roughly 90 calories per scoop, from a blend of chicken and grass-fed beef broth, per the label at the time of writing. That is 50 percent more protein per scoop, generally at a lower cost per 25 g. The trade-off is a blended source and a more processed concentrate, which some paleo buyers specifically avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            Naked Bone Broth is the minimalist rival. Naked Nutrition lists beef broth protein concentrate with a little sunflower lecithin, about 20 to 21 g of protein per two-scoop serving and European bone sourcing, per its listings. It is a good choice if you want an unflavored powder with a short label and more protein per serving, though buyer feedback on mixing is mixed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paleovalley wins on the specific sourcing and processing story: grass-fed and grass-finished beef only, simmered in water, no solvents, and organic flavor ingredients with monk fruit. It loses on protein per scoop and on price. Choose it if those sourcing details are your deciding factor; choose one of the rivals if you want more protein for less money from the same category.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a full ranking, our <a href="/best-bone-broth-protein-powder/">best bone broth protein powder guide</a> scores five products on protein density, sourcing and cost. If you have decided broth is not the point and you simply want a clean label, the <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">clean protein powder picks</a> cover whey and plant options with short ingredient lists and much better protein numbers.
          </p>
        </div>

