            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Paleovalley Bone Broth Protein good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a good bone broth powder and a poor general protein powder. The label is short, the beef bones are grass-fed and grass-finished per the brand, and the flavored versions use only monk fruit to sweeten. But at about 13 g of collagen-heavy protein per scoop and over $3 per 25 g of protein, it is best used as an add-in rather than a main shake.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein is in Paleovalley Bone Broth Protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 13 g per scoop, per the brand's Amazon listings and retailer copies of the label at the time of writing. Some older third-party listings show up to 15 g for Unflavored. The Chocolate label lists about 80 calories per scoop. Check the tub you buy, because figures vary slightly by flavor and the brand updates recipes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Paleovalley Bone Broth Protein the same as collagen?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not exactly. It is a dried beef bone broth, so most of its protein is collagen, but it is made by simmering bones in water rather than hydrolyzing hides into collagen peptides. That gives it a more broth-like character and explains why it dissolves less completely. Nutritionally, treat it like collagen: rich in glycine and proline, low in leucine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use Paleovalley Bone Broth Protein to build muscle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It counts toward your daily protein, but it is a weak choice for building muscle on its own. Collagen lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, the amino acid that triggers muscle protein synthesis. If muscle is your goal, use whey, soy or a pea and rice blend for your main doses, and add Paleovalley on top if you enjoy it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does Paleovalley Bone Broth Protein taste like?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
